  <title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01219</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3853d146-ae3f-439a-aadf-14272a8d0a15</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">  <title>Question #1: Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>

      <para id="para-00001">Which function could represent the graph?</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_03_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
      <para id="para-00003">You can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. You can also do the reverse—identify the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">
        
          <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, negative 4), passes through the plotted point (3, negative 2), and intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (4, 0).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00006">To find the slope, choose two points on the graph. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, and the graph passes through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mi>i</m:mi><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mi>e</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mi>u</m:mi><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
      <para id="para-00011">  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute the values into <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00015"><emphasis>Step 6 -
 </emphasis> Rewrite the equation using function notation.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><note class="try" id="note-00001">
  <title>Try It: Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00017">Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">
              
                <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 9 to 9. The point (3, negative 1) is plotted. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 2), intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (9 fourths, 0), and passes through the point (3, negative 1).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Here is how to write a linear function from a graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Choose two points from the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00023">    <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mi>i</m:mi><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mi>e</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mi>u</m:mi><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00026"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00029">Step 5 - 
  Substitute the values into <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00031">
             <emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> 
          </para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Rewrite the equation using function notation.</para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="d7bebde0-c39c-4cbb-8bc8-e287086de9df" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00035">Which linear function could represent the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00036">
              
                <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (negative 2, 0), intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 1) and passes through the plotted point (5, 4).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00037">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! Check yourself: The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Use the points <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> to find the slope, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Try again. Let’s try again a different way: Notice that the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Use the points <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> to find the slope.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
